            {
              "type": "reasoning.summary",
              "summary": "**Evaluating survey confidence**\n\nI’m thinking about how confidence in reports doesn’t guarantee accuracy, and overlapping surveys might complicate matters. Distinct surveys are likely designed to count each observation only once, which suggests they expect us to infer an aggregate from all reports. \n\nIf peer confidence reflects probabilities in their recommendations, combining their evidence with my own means I need an equivalent posterior confidence for their surveys. It’s interesting how the statement “each observation counted once” emphasizes the importance of careful interpretation to avoid misleading aggregations.**Exploring confidence in surveys**\n\nI'm trying to identify whether the fraction .685715 represents a posterior confidence based on three survey observations. I wonder if the accuracy of the surveys matters—like if my prior confidence stems from recommendations based on tallies. \n\nIt seems I could interpret the posterior confidence based on majority signals as .6, and for a unanimous decision, it could be .7714. Still, I need to consider how random ties might affect recommendations. Ultimately, it looks like the explicit warning encourages me not to equate confidence with truth and to treat it as soft information.",
